
/*
PROBLEM: COUNT DISTINCT VALUES
=================================================

PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:

We are given an array containing n integers.

Our task is to calculate the number of distinct values
present in the array.

Distinct values means values that appear only once
when we consider unique values.

For example:

arr = [2, 3, 2, 2, 3]

Distinct values are:

2 and 3

Therefore, the answer is 2.


APPROACH: SORTING + TWO POINTERS
=================================================

STEP 1:
Sort the array in ascending order.

After sorting, all duplicate elements will come together.

Example:

Original array:

[2, 3, 2, 2, 3]

After sorting:

[2, 2, 2, 3, 3]


STEP 2:
We use two variables:

i = 0
j = 1

Here:

i points to the last unique element.
j is used to traverse the remaining array.


STEP 3:
Compare arr[i] and arr[j].

CASE 1:
If arr[i] == arr[j]

Both elements are equal.

This means arr[j] is a duplicate.

We do not increase i.

We only increase j through the loop.


CASE 2:
If arr[i] != arr[j]

We have found a new distinct element.

We store this new element in:

arr[i + 1] = arr[j]

Then increase i.

This makes i point to the newly found distinct element.


STEP 4:
After the loop finishes:

i represents the index of the last distinct element.

Since array indexing starts from 0:

Number of distinct elements = i + 1


WHY THIS APPROACH WORKS:

Sorting places all duplicate elements together.

Therefore, whenever arr[i] and arr[j] are different,
we have found a new distinct value.

We move that value to the next position
in the beginning of the array.

Finally, i + 1 gives the total number
of distinct values.


EXAMPLE:

arr = [2, 3, 2, 2, 3]

After sorting:

arr = [2, 2, 2, 3, 3]

Initially:

i = 0

j = 1

We compare arr[i] and arr[j].

If they are equal, we skip the duplicate.

If they are different, we store the new value
at arr[i + 1] and increase i.

After completing the traversal:

i = 1

Therefore:

Answer = i + 1 = 2


TIME COMPLEXITY:

Sorting:
O(n log n)

Traversing:
O(n)

Overall:
O(n log n)


AUXILIARY SPACE COMPLEXITY:

O(1) extra space for the two-pointer traversal,
excluding sorting implementation details.

Note:
The given function modifies the original array.
*/


#include <iostream>
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;


// FUNCTION: COUNT DISTINCT VALUES
// =================================================
//
// n      = number of elements in the array
// arr[]  = array containing the given elements
//
// RETURN:
// Number of distinct values present in the array

int f(int n, int arr[])
{

    // STEP 1:
    // Sort the array in ascending order.
    //
    // After sorting, duplicate elements
    // will come together.
    //
    // Example:
    //
    // Before sorting:
    // [2, 3, 2, 2, 3]
    //
    // After sorting:
    // [2, 2, 2, 3, 3]

    sort(arr, arr + n);


    // STEP 2:
    // Initialize i to 0.
    //
    // i represents the index of the last
    // distinct element found.
    //
    // Initially, arr[0] is considered
    // the first distinct element.

    int i = 0;


    // STEP 3:
    // Traverse the array using j.
    //
    // We start j from 1 because arr[0]
    // is already considered a distinct element.
    //
    // j moves from index 1 to index n - 1.

    for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
    {

        // STEP 4:
        // Store the element at index i.
        //
        // This is the last distinct element
        // found so far.

        int start = arr[i];


        // Store the current element at index j.
        //
        // We compare this element with
        // the last distinct element.

        int end = arr[j];


        // CASE 1:
        // If both elements are different,
        // we have found a new distinct value.

        if (start != end)
        {

            // STEP 5:
            // Store the new distinct element
            // at the next position after i.
            //
            // This moves the distinct element
            // towards the beginning of the array.
            //
            // Example:
            //
            // arr = [2, 2, 2, 3, 3]
            //
            // arr[i + 1] = arr[j]
            //
            // arr = [2, 3, 2, 3, 3]

            arr[i + 1] = arr[j];


            // STEP 6:
            // Increase i.
            //
            // Now i points to the newly
            // found distinct element.
            //
            // This element becomes the
            // reference for the next comparison.

            i++;

        }

        // CASE 2:
        // If start == end
        //
        // The current element is a duplicate.
        //
        // We do not need to do anything.
        //
        // The loop automatically increases j
        // and checks the next element.

    }


    // STEP 7:
    // Return the number of distinct elements.
    //
    // i represents the index of the last
    // distinct element.
    //
    // Since indexing starts from 0:
    //
    // Number of distinct elements = i + 1

    return i + 1;

}


// MAIN FUNCTION
// =================================================

int main()
{

    // STEP 1:
    // Take the number of elements in the array.

    int n;
    cin >> n;


    // STEP 2:
    // Declare an array of size n.
    //
    // This array will store the given elements.

    int arr[n];


    // STEP 3:
    // Take n integers as input.
    //
    // Store each integer inside the array.

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {

        // Take one element as input.

        int ele;
        cin >> ele;


        // Store the element at index i.

        arr[i] = ele;

    }


    // STEP 4:
    // Call the function f().
    //
    // Pass:
    //
    // n     = number of elements
    // arr   = array containing the elements
    //
    // The function returns the number
    // of distinct values.

    cout << f(n, arr) << endl;


    // STEP 5:
    // End the program.

    return 0;

}

